/* =============================================================================
   ONE-OFF: seed 'RolePreference Alignments' with DUMMY alignments
   Paste onto a temporary button's OnSelect, press it once, then delete the
   button. Safe to re-run: it only adds a row for someone who has none, so it
   never disturbs a decision that has already been submitted.

   WHY: Kate/Claire supply the real assigned role and the real 150-word
   reasoning through the PAB-6118 spreadsheet (docs/PAB-6118-export.md), and
   that is not due until the preference window closes. This gives the alignment
   screens something to render in the meantime, and gives UAT someone to click
   through as.

   WHAT IT DOES: every person on 'RolePreference People' gets one row proposing
   a role, with a placeholder reasoning attached. The role is their own rank-1
   preference where they have one; where they have not submitted preferences at
   all it falls back to a DefaultOption role, then to the first Active role -
   the same chain OnStart uses to build colRanks. That fallback matters for
   testing: without it anyone who has not filled in the preference form gets no
   alignment row, so the Role Alignment card stays shut and there is nothing to
   click. That is almost certainly your own test account.

   Both Accept and Reject are reachable from any seeded row - the decision does
   not depend on which role was assigned - so this one seed exercises the whole
   flow. To re-test after deciding, use reset-alignment-decision.powerfx.

   >>> REPLACE WITH REAL DATA BEFORE GO-LIVE. Anything with "Placeholder" in
   >>> AssignedReason has not been written by Kate or Claire. Search the column
   >>> for that word to prove none of it survived.
   ============================================================================ */

/* JUST ME, FASTER: to seed only the signed-in user instead of all ~105 people,
   replace the ForAll(...) call below with the single Collect(...) it contains,
   substituting varUser.EmpId for every p.EmployeeID. One row, one API call,
   enough to walk the whole flow. */

/* DESTRUCTIVE, opt-in. Uncomment the next line to wipe and rebuild every
   alignment row - this also throws away any decisions people have already
   submitted, so never run it once the alignment window has opened.
   RemoveIf('RolePreference Alignments', true); */

ForAll(
    'RolePreference People' As p,
    With(
        {
            /* Same fallback chain as OnStart section 3, so a person with no
               submitted ranking still gets a testable row. */
            k1: Coalesce(
                LookUp('RolePreference Preferences', EmployeeID = p.EmployeeID And Rank = 1).RoleKey,
                First(Filter('RolePreference Roles', Active And DefaultOption)).RoleKey,
                First(Filter('RolePreference Roles', Active)).RoleKey
            ),
            existing: LookUp('RolePreference Alignments', EmployeeID = p.EmployeeID)
        },
        If(
            Not IsBlank(p.EmployeeID) And Not IsBlank(k1) And IsBlank(existing.EmployeeID),
            Collect(
                'RolePreference Alignments',
                {
                    EmployeeID: p.EmployeeID,
                    AssignedRoleKey: k1,
                    AssignedRoleName: Coalesce(LookUp('RolePreference Roles', RoleKey = k1).RoleName, "[missing role: " & k1 & "]"),
                    AssignedReason: "Placeholder reasoning — the final 150 words come from Kate and Claire via the PAB-6118 spreadsheet. Your ranking, your supporting answers and the shape of the new structure were considered together when this role was matched to you. You ranked this role highly and the experience you described maps onto its accountabilities. The panel also weighed the number of posts available against the preferences expressed across the whole area, and the balance of skills the new team needs from day one. On that basis this role is the best available match for your experience and for the programme, and it keeps you working with the relationships you have already built. If anything here does not look right, speak to your HR Business Partner before you respond.",
                    Decision: "",
                    RejectReasons: "",
                    RejectComments: "",
                    Status: ""
                }
            )
        )
    )
);
Notify("Alignments seeded: " & CountRows('RolePreference Alignments') & " rows. Run OnStart to pick them up.", NotificationType.Success)
